<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Lab 1, Due: Monday, January 29</title>
  <introduction>
    <p><term>Instructions:</term> The exercises here should be completed in groups
of 2 or 3 students.  You only need to hand in one copy per group, but
please include everyone's name.</p>
    <p>There are two aims for this lab.  First, I want to introduce
you to spreadsheets as a tool for understanding some ideas in calculus.  At
the beginning of the class, I will demonstrate for you some
fundamental things: how to enter data in cells, how to refer to other
cells and how to copy information from one cell to another.  Second,
I would like to review how definite integrals are
constructed;  namely, I would like for
you to think of an integral as a fancy sum.</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>%</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Your first task is to create an Excel worksheet to
%   add together the first 100 <em>odd</em> numbers.  In the first column,
%   you should list the odd numbers 1, 3, 5, ... The best way to do this
%   is to enter ``1'' in cell <term>A1</term>.  Then enter ``=A1 + 2'' in cell
%   <term>A2</term> and copy this down into the next 98 rows.</p>
        <p>%   In the second column, you will want to start with ``1'' in cell <term>%   B1</term>---this is the first odd integer.  Now in cell <term>B2</term>, enter
%   ``=B1 + A2''---this gives the sum of the first
%   2 odd numbers.  You can now copy this into the next 98 rows.</p>
        <p>%   What is the sum of the first 100 odd integers?  Make sure you
%   understand how the spreadsheet is set up to compute this sum.</p>
        <p>%</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>%   If you start at the
%   top of the second column and look down, you will see the sum of the
%   first <m>n</m> integers in cell <term>Bn</term>.  What pattern do you notice?</p>
        <p>%</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Remember that if
we have a function <m>f(x)</m>, we can define the definite integral
<md>
\int_a^b f(x)~dx
</md>
by looking at either left sums <m>L_n</m> or right sums <m>R_n</m>.  To form
these, we choose some value for <m>n</m> and define <m>\Delta x =
\frac{b-a}{n}</m> and the points <m>x_i=a+i\Delta x</m>.  The left and right
Riemann sums are 
<md>
<mrow>L_n\amp=\ampf(x_0)\Delta x + f(x_1)\Delta x + \ldots + f(x_{n-1})\Delta x =
\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}f(x_i)\Delta x</mrow>
<mrow>R_n\amp=\ampf(x_1)\Delta x + f(x_2)\Delta x + \ldots + f(x_{n})\Delta x =
\sum_{i=1}^nf(x_i)\Delta x</mrow>
</md>
The definite integral is then defined as 
<md>
\int_a^b f(x)~dx  =\lim_{n\to\infty}L_n = \lim_{n\to\infty}R_n.
</md></p>
        <p>Let's now consider the function <m>f(x) = x^3</m> and
the definite integral
<md>
\int_0^1 x^3~dx.
</md>
First off, use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate this
integral exactly.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Set up a Google sheet as shown below to evaluate the
Riemann sums with <m>n = 10</m>.  The first column is <m>\Delta x</m>.  The
second column starts at <m>0</m> and each subsequent entry is obtained by
adding <m>\Delta x</m> to the entry above.  This generates the points
<m>x_i</m>.  The third column is simply the function <m>f(x)=x^3</m> evaluated
at <m>x_i</m>.  The fourth column will add the terms for the left sum, and
the fifth column will add the terms for the right sum.  To obtain the
entry in one row, simply add the appropriate term <m>f(x_i)\Delta x</m> to
the entry directly above.</p>
        <p>A convenient way to set this up is to enter
0.1 for <m>\Delta x</m> in cell <term>A2</term>, enter ``=A2'' in cell <term>A3</term>
and copy that entry into the rest of the column.  That way, if you
change <m>\Delta x</m> in one place, it will change everywhere.</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
<m>\Delta x</m> &amp; <m>x_i</m> &amp; <m>f(x_i)</m>  &amp; <m>L_n</m> &amp; <m>R_n</m> 
\hline
  0.1000&amp;0.0000&amp;0.0000&amp;  &amp; 
  0.1000&amp;0.1000&amp;0.0010&amp;0.0000&amp;0.0001
  0.1000&amp;0.2000&amp;0.0080&amp;0.0001&amp;0.0009
  0.1000&amp;0.3000&amp;0.0270&amp;0.0009&amp;0.0036
\vdots &amp; \vdots &amp; \vdots &amp; \vdots &amp; 
\hline
\end{tabular}</p>
        <p>What do you find for the Riemann sums, <m>L_{10}</m> and <m>R_{10}</m>?  Be sure
that you have actually 
included exactly 10 terms in your approximations.  State your results
with 6 digits after the decimal place.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now repeat this using <m>n = 100</m>.  This corresponds to <m>\Delta x = 0.01</m>.
If you set up your earlier worksheet well, you should only need to change
the entry for <m>\Delta x</m> in one location.  What do you find for the
Riemann sums?  State your result with 6 digits after the decimal place.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Repeat using <m>n = 1000</m>. What do you find for the Riemann sums?
State your result with 6 digits after the decimal place.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Shown below is the graph of the function <m>f(x)=x^3</m>.  Sketch in the
rectangles you used in building the left Riemann sum with <m>\Delta x = 0.1</m>.
Then use your figure to explain why the approximations <m>L_n</m> and <m>R_n</m>
to the definite integral you found above are either too large or too
small.</p>
        <p><image source="figures/g2c.eps"/></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Now fill in the following table:</p>
        <p>\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
<m>\Delta x</m> &amp; <m>L_n</m> &amp; Error 
\hline
0.1 \vphantom{\Huge p}
&amp;  &amp; 
0.01 \vphantom{\Huge p}
&amp; &amp; 
0.001 \vphantom{\Huge p}
&amp; &amp; 
\hline
\end{tabular}</p>
        <p>What do you notice about the error?  Try to be quantitative rather
than just qualitative; that is, when the width of the intervals
<m>\Delta x</m>
decreases by a factor of one tenth, by roughly what factor does the
error decrease?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>The definite integral <md>\int_0^1e^{-x^2}~dx</md> cannot
  be evaluated using the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus because there is
  not an antiderivative that can be found in terms of familiar
  functions.</p>
      <p>Using left and right Riemann sums, give an estimate of this
  definite integral with an error of no more than <m>0.005</m>.  Explain
  how you've done this with left and right Riemann sums.  Do not
  evaluate the integral on your calculator to estimate the error.</p>
      <p>Note:  to evalue an exponential in a spreadsheet, use ``= EXP( ... )''.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
